james-will-2.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps james-will-2.logic.r1.2 and james-will-2.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "the necessary acts we erroneously regret are good", "our error in so regretting them is also good", "possibly, (the necessary acts we erroneously regret are good and our error in so regretting them is also good)" and "possibly, (the necessary acts we erroneously regret are good and our error in so regretting them is also good)" are true and "the world is regarded not as a machine for making real any outward good but as a contrivance for deepening the theoretic consciousness of what goodness and evil intrinsically are" is false. Is S2, "The necessary acts we erroneously regret {165} may be good, and yet our error in so regretting them may be also good, on one simple condition; and that condition is this: The world must not be regarded as a machine whose final purpose is the making real of any outward good, but rather as a contrivance for deepening the theoretic consciousness of what goodness and evil in their intrinsic natures are.", true then?
james-will-2.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: james-will-2.logic.r1 and james-will-2.logic.r1.2 have "cares_knowing ∧ ¬cares_doing → gnostic"; james-will-2.logic.r1.3 has "gnostic → acts_good ∧ regret_good" and "cares_knowing ∧ ¬cares_doing → gnostic"; they differ when only "the world is regarded not as a machine for making real any outward good but as a contrivance for deepening the theoretic consciousness of what goodness and evil intrinsically are" and "the necessary acts we erroneously regret are good" are true, which the first allows
